Article 7 Annual and semi-annual financial accounting reports shall include:
(1) accounting statements;
(2) notes to accounting statements;
(3) financial condition statement.
Accounting statements shall include a balance sheet, an income statement, a cash flow statement, and related supplementary schedules.

Article 14 The notes to accounting statements are explanations of the basis of preparation, basis of compilation, principles and methods of preparation, and major items of the accounting statements, to facilitate the users' understanding of the content of the accounting statements. The notes to accounting statements shall at least include the following:
(1) explanations of departure from basic accounting assumptions;
(2) information on significant accounting policies and accounting estimates, including changes thereto, reasons for such changes, and their impact on the financial position and operating results;
(3) explanations of contingent events and events after the balance sheet date;
(4) explanations of related party relationships and transactions;
(5) information on the transfer and sale of significant assets;
(6) business combinations and divisions;
(7) major investment and financing activities;
(8) detailed information on major items in the accounting statements;
(9) other items that need to be explained to facilitate understanding and analysis of the accounting statements.

Article 15 The financial condition statement shall at least provide explanations on the following:
(1) the basic situation of the enterprise's production and operation;
(2) the realization and distribution of profits;
(3) the increase, decrease, and turnover of funds;
(4) other matters that have a significant impact on the financial position, operating results, and cash flows of the enterprise.

Article 20 Before preparing an annual financial accounting report, an enterprise shall conduct a comprehensive check of assets and verify liabilities in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) Verify whether settlement items, including accounts receivable, accounts payable, and taxes payable, exist and whether the corresponding amounts with debtor and creditor units are consistent;
(2) Verify whether the physical quantities of raw materials, work in process, semi-finished goods, finished goods, and other inventories match the book quantities, and whether there are losses from scrapping and overstocked materials;
(3) Verify whether various investments exist and whether investment income is recognized and measured in accordance with the uniform accounting system of the State;
(4) Verify whether the physical quantities of buildings, machinery, equipment, transport vehicles, and other fixed assets match the book quantities;
(5) Verify whether the actual amounts incurred for construction in progress match the book records;
(6) Other items requiring checking and verification.
Through the checking and verification prescribed in the preceding paragraph, the enterprise shall ascertain whether the physical quantities of property and materials match the book quantities, the delinquencies on various settlement items and their causes, the actual reserve situation of materials and supplies, whether various investments have achieved their intended objectives, the usage and condition of fixed assets, and other matters. After checking and verification, the enterprise shall report the results of the checking and verification and the measures for handling them to the board of directors or the corresponding body of the enterprise, and shall make corresponding accounting treatments in accordance with the provisions of the uniform accounting system of the State.
An enterprise shall, during the fiscal year, conduct focused inspections, rotating checks, or periodic checks of various property and materials and settlement items as appropriate to the circumstances.

Article 21 Before preparing a financial accounting report, an enterprise shall, in addition to conducting a comprehensive check of assets and verifying liabilities, complete the following work:
(1) Reconcile the content and amounts of each accounting book record with the accounting vouchers to ensure consistency, and verify that the debit and credit directions are correct;
(2) Close the accounts on the closing date prescribed in these Regulations, calculate the balances and current amounts of the relevant accounting books, and reconcile the balances among the accounting books;
(3) Check whether the relevant accounting treatments are carried out in accordance with the uniform accounting system of the State;
(4) For transactions and events for which the uniform accounting system of the State does not prescribe a uniform accounting method, check whether the recognition and measurement are made in accordance with the general principles of accounting and whether the relevant accounting entries are reasonable;
(5) Check whether there are items in the current period or previous periods that need adjustment due to accounting errors, changes in accounting policies, or other reasons.
If problems are found during the work prescribed in the preceding paragraph, they shall be handled in accordance with the provisions of the uniform accounting system of the State.

Article 35 State-owned enterprises, enterprises that are state-owned or under state control or where the state holds a dominant position shall disclose their financial accounting reports to the workers' congress of the enterprise at least once a year, and shall provide focused explanations on the following matters:
(1) Information closely related to the interests of the employees, including: the composition of administrative expenses; the distribution, use, and balance of salaries and welfare of enterprise management personnel and employees; the appropriation and use of public welfare funds; the distribution of profits; and other information related to the interests of employees;
(2) Problems discovered during internal audits and the status of their correction;
(3) The audit status by certified public accountants;
(4) Problems discovered by state audit authorities and the status of their correction;
(5) Explanations of major investment, financing, and asset disposal decisions and their reasons;
(6) Other important items that need to be explained.

Article 39 Where any of the following acts is committed in violation of these Regulations, the finance department of the people's government at or above the county level shall order rectification within a prescribed time limit, and may impose a fine of not less than 3,000 yuan but not more than 50,000 yuan on the enterprise; and may impose a fine of not less than 2,000 yuan but not more than 20,000 yuan on the directly responsible persons in charge and other directly responsible persons; if the person is a state functionary, administrative or disciplinary sanctions shall also be imposed according to law:
(1) arbitrarily changing the recognition and measurement standards for accounting elements;
(2) arbitrarily changing the basis of preparation, basis of compilation, principles, or methods of financial accounting reports;
(3) closing accounts before or after the prescribed closing date;
(4) failing to comprehensively check assets and verify liabilities in accordance with these Regulations before preparing an annual financial accounting report;
(5) refusing supervision and inspection lawfully conducted by the finance department or other relevant departments on financial accounting reports, or failing to provide truthful information.
If an accounting staff member commits any of the acts listed in the preceding paragraph and the circumstances are serious, the finance department of the people's government at or above the county level shall revoke the accounting practitioner's qualification certificate.
